%U A341314 1,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,1,1,12,6,4,3,12,2,12,3,4,6,12,1
%N A341314 Array read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) = (n+k)/gcd(n,k), n>=0, k>=0.
%C A341314 We define gcd(0,0) = 0.
%C A341314 This sequence arose when studying Reed Kelly's A214551.
%C A341314 From _Tomas Laris_, Nov 12 2026: (Start)
%C A341314 For n >= 1, the n-th antidiagonal is the palindromic row (n/gcd(n,k))_{k=0..n}, since gcd(n,k) = gcd(n,n-k).
%C A341314 For n >= 1, these entries are the additive orders of the residue classes k modulo n, with k = n representing the zero class. For n >= 2, exactly phi(n) entries in the n-th antidiagonal are equal to n.
%C A341314 The sum of the entries in the n-th antidiagonal is 1 + A057660(n), for n >= 1. (End)
%H A341314 Tomas Laris, <a href="/A341314/a341314.png">Symmetric triangular representation of A341314: Palindromic antidiagonals</a> (2026).
%F A341314 For n >= 1 and 0 <= k <= n, T(k,n-k) = n/gcd(n,k) = n/gcd(n,n-k). - _Tomas Laris_, Nov 12 2026
%e A341314 The array begins:
